  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 409 AM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nMuch colder air continues to move into the Upper Midwest behind\nyesterday's cold front, with below-normal temperatures \ncontinuing into the weekend. Cyclonic flow aloft behind the\nfront is generating enough lift for isolated snow \nshowers through the morning, with the greatest coverage across \nwestern Wisconsin. Elsewhere woudn't be surprised to see a few \nflurries through the morning. Patches of clouds will stick \naround in the cold air advection regime with temperatures not \nexpected to warm out of the upper 30s this afternoon. The clouds\nlikely stick around overnight which may keep temperatures a few\ndegrees warmer than the general model consensus. If clouds \nstick around, expect lows Friday morning in the mid to upper \n20s, low to mid 20s if we are able to clear out. Drier high \npressure centers over the region Friday & Saturday along with \nour coldest air of the season. Highs again will only range from \nthe mid 30s to low 40s, with clearer skies allowing for \novernight lows to fall well into the 20s, if not a few teens in \nspots across central Minnesota.\n\nA noticeable warming trend develops next week as upper-level\nridging builds over the central CONUS and southerly flow\ndevelops on the back side of the departing high pressure.\nAbove-normal temepratures return for Monday & the warming trend\nincreases through the week, with ensemble guidance suggesting \ntemperature anomalies of 15-20 F above normal for Wednesday & \nThursday. Most of that abnormal warmth will come from the warm\novernight lows, but still expect some highs in the upper 50s, if\nnow 60s, for midweek. Breezy southerly winds will also accompany\nthe warming trend as the pressure gradient tightens over the \nUpper Midwest, although there doesn't appear to be much of a \nsignal for strong gusts of 35-40+ mph. Dry weather persists \nthrough most of the week under the surface high pressure & \npronounced upper-level ridging, with the next shot at any \nappreciable rainfall not arriving until Thursday-Friday at the \nearliest.",
